{
 "total": 69.397,
 "scenes": [
  {
   "v": "hook_lice",
   "start": 0.0,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Scientists used lice to work out when people started wearing clothes.",
     "start": 0.6,
     "end": 4.285
    },
    {
     "text": "Yes. Lice.",
     "start": 4.605,
     "end": 5.503
    }
   ],
   "end": 5.873
  },
  {
   "v": "hook_q",
   "start": 5.873,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "So how did cavemen stay warm?",
     "start": 6.173,
     "end": 7.889
    }
   ],
   "end": 8.259
  },
  {
   "v": "meet_moss",
   "start": 8.259,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Meet Moss. He's freezing.",
     "start": 8.559,
     "end": 10.004
    }
   ],
   "end": 10.374
  },
  {
   "v": "body_lice",
   "start": 10.374,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Surprise one: body lice live in clothes.",
     "start": 10.674,
     "end": 13.067
    },
    {
     "text": "Their DNA suggests they split from head lice at least eighty-three thousand years ago.",
     "start": 13.387,
     "end": 18.246
    },
    {
     "text": "Maybe earlier.",
     "start": 18.566,
     "end": 19.518
    }
   ],
   "end": 19.888
  },
  {
   "v": "skins",
   "start": 19.888,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "So clothes are old.",
     "start": 20.188,
     "end": 21.409
    },
    {
     "text": "They were likely made from animal skins and fur.",
     "start": 21.729,
     "end": 24.151
    }
   ],
   "end": 24.521
  },
  {
   "v": "needles",
   "start": 24.521,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Surprise two: bone needles!",
     "start": 24.821,
     "end": 26.543
    },
    {
     "text": "The oldest eyed needles come from Siberia.",
     "start": 26.863,
     "end": 29.262
    },
    {
     "text": "They're about forty thousand years old, maybe more.",
     "start": 29.582,
     "end": 32.114
    }
   ],
   "end": 32.484
  },
  {
   "v": "needle_eye",
   "start": 32.484,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "A tiny hole for thread, carved into bone.",
     "start": 32.784,
     "end": 35.273
    }
   ],
   "end": 35.643
  },
  {
   "v": "layers",
   "start": 35.643,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Surprise three: needles may mean layers.",
     "start": 35.943,
     "end": 38.262
    },
    {
     "text": "Some scientists even wonder about Stone Age underwear!",
     "start": 38.582,
     "end": 41.826
    }
   ],
   "end": 42.196
  },
  {
   "v": "sungir",
   "start": 42.196,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Surprise four: in Russia, over thirty thousand years ago, three people were buried wearing ivory beads.",
     "start": 42.496,
     "end": 48.706
    },
    {
     "text": "More than thirteen thousand of them!",
     "start": 49.026,
     "end": 50.821
    }
   ],
   "end": 51.191
  },
  {
   "v": "bead_hours",
   "start": 51.191,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Many seem to have been sewn onto their clothes.",
     "start": 51.491,
     "end": 53.634
    },
    {
     "text": "Making them all took thousands of hours.",
     "start": 53.954,
     "end": 56.137
    }
   ],
   "end": 56.507
  },
  {
   "v": "moss_sews",
   "start": 56.507,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "And Moss? He tried sewing. He stitched his tunic to his leg.",
     "start": 56.807,
     "end": 60.122
    }
   ],
   "end": 60.492
  },
  {
   "v": "ending",
   "start": 60.492,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "So Stone Age people stayed warm with fur, fire and clever sewing.",
     "start": 60.792,
     "end": 64.673
    }
   ],
   "end": 65.043
  },
  {
   "v": "follow",
   "start": 65.043,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Follow for more Stone Age Stories.",
     "start": 65.343,
     "end": 67.227
    }
   ],
   "end": 69.397
  }
 ]
}